{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_12-22-18-hashicorp_raft-real-run/agent-source && sed -n '140,160p' config.go; echo \\\"=== randomTimeout/minCheckInterval\\\"; grep -n \\\"func randomTimeout\\\" -A 8 util.go; grep -rn \\\"minCheckInterval\\\" --include=*.go . | grep -v _test\"","aggregated_output":"\t// Raft servers running an older version of the code. This is used to\n\t// version the wire protocol as well as Raft-specific log entries that\n\t// the server uses when _speaking_ to other servers. There is currently\n\t// no auto-negotiation of versions so all servers must be manually\n\t// configured with compatible versions. See ProtocolVersionMin and\n\t// ProtocolVersionMax for the versions of the protocol that this server\n\t// can _understand_.\n\tProtocolVersion ProtocolVersion\n\n\t// HeartbeatTimeout specifies the time in follower state without contact\n\t// from a leader before we attempt an election.\n\tHeartbeatTimeout time.Duration\n\n\t// ElectionTimeout specifies the time in candidate state without contact\n\t// from a leader before we attempt an election.\n\tElectionTimeout time.Duration\n\n\t// CommitTimeout specifies the time without an Apply operation before the\n\t// leader sends an AppendEntry RPC to followers, to ensure a timely commit of\n\t// log entries.\n\t// Due to random staggering, may be delayed as much as 2x this value.\n=== randomTimeout/minCheckInterval\n29:func randomTimeout(minVal time.Duration) <-chan time.Time {\n30-\tif minVal == 0 {\n31-\t\treturn nil\n32-\t}\n33-\textra := time.Duration(rand.Int63()) % minVal\n34-\treturn time.After(minVal + extra)\n35-}\n36-\n37-// min returns the minimum.\n./raft.go:22:\tminCheckInterval          = 10 * time.Millisecond\n./raft.go:943:\t\t\tif checkInterval < minCheckInterval {\n./raft.go:944:\t\t\t\tcheckInterval = minCheckInterval\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_12-22-18-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft && python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nst=json.load(open('../state.json'))\nrec=[i for i in st['interpretations'] if i.get('id')=='bc29f42a57d34a7bb2748bc0c137b09c-interpretation-0'][0]\n\nlim=[l for l in rec['limitations'] if not l.startswith('The stale-read consequence')]\nnew_lim=(\\\"The stale-read consequence needs a successor elected while the old leader reports verify success, and the timing that decides it is now read from source rather than assumed. A follower attempts an election after HeartbeatTimeout without leader contact (config.go: HeartbeatTimeout is 'the time in follower state without contact from a leader before we attempt an election') and randomTimeout adds a random fraction, so it campaigns in [H, 2H); ElectionTimeout only governs a candidate's retry. The leader's lease check runs every max(10ms, LeaderLeaseTimeout - maxDiff) and steps down at the first check whose voter contact age exceeds LeaderLeaseTimeout. ValidateConfig enforces LeaderLeaseTimeout <= HeartbeatTimeout, so with the documented defaults (500ms and 1000ms) the leader leaves the leader state roughly 500ms before the earliest campaign, and the two windows cannot overlap. They can touch when LeaderLeaseTimeout == HeartbeatTimeout, which validation permits: the last check passes while the age is exactly LeaderLeaseTimeout (the comparison is diff <= leaseTimeout), and the step-down then waits for the next check, at least minCheckInterval = 10ms away, while a voter is already allowed to be campaigning. That leaves a source-derived exposure of up to about 10ms plus whatever the election itself takes, and a longer one if the leader's own goroutine is stalled or its clock jumps, which is what the earlier account relied on.\\\")\ninterp={\n \\\"investigation_id\\\":\\\"3b823834c8cb46da98e77d8e988bf5d9-question-0\\\",\n \\\"source_ids\\\":list(dict.fromkeys(rec['source_ids']+[\\\"follower_election_trigger\\\",\\\"follower_campaign_logic\\\",\\\"lease_check_granularity\\\",\\\"random_timeout\\\"])),\n \\\"executions\\\":rec['executions'],\n \\\"conclusion\\\":rec['conclusion'],\n \\\"defect_claim\\\":rec['defect_claim'],\n \\\"rationale\\\":rec['rationale'],\n \\\"knowledge\\\":rec['knowledge']+[{\\\"entry_id\\\":\\\"@follower_election_trigger_semantics\\\",\\\"revision\\\":1,\n   \\\"assertion\\\":\\\"A follower campaigns after HeartbeatTimeout without leader contact, not after ElectionTimeout, and the leader's lease check granularity is max(10ms, LeaderLeaseTimeout - maxDiff).\\\"}],\n \\\"answers\\\":{ \\\"d704b04e706144dc964ff451359eda19-gap-0\\\":{\n   \\\"explanation\\\":(\\\"Still unresolved, with a narrower condition set than the earlier answer. Configuration routes are closed by source: checkLeaderLease counts voters only, and both ValidateConfig and ReloadConfig keep LeaderLeaseTimeout <= HeartbeatTimeout. Reading the timings shows why the two windows normally cannot overlap: a follower campaigns after HeartbeatTimeout without contact, with randomTimeout stretching it to [H, 2H), while the leader steps down at the first lease check whose voter contact age exceeds LeaderLeaseTimeout. The exception is LeaderLeaseTimeout == HeartbeatTimeout, which validation permits: the check whose measured age is exactly LeaderLeaseTimeout still counts the voter as contacted, and the step-down then waits for the next check at least minCheckInterval = 10ms later, so a voter may already be campaigning while the old leader still counts a non-voter's acknowledgement; the resulting exposure is about 10ms plus the election itself. No execution has observed that window, so the gap stays open and needs either a probe that hits the boundary or a leader-side stall.\\\"),\n   \\\"resolved\\\":False}},\n \\\"counterevidence\\\":rec['counterevidence'],\n \\\"limitations\\\":[new_lim]+lim}\n\nknowledge={\n \\\"ref\\\":\\\"@follower_election_trigger_semantics\\\",\n \\\"kind\\\":\\\"behavior\\\",\n \\\"statement\\\":\\\"A follower attempts an election after HeartbeatTimeout without contact from a leader, and ElectionTimeout governs only a candidate's retry. runFollower arms heartbeatTimer with randomTimeout(HeartbeatTimeout) and campaigns when the time since last contact is at least HeartbeatTimeout, while runCandidate arms its timer with randomTimeout(ElectionTimeout) and simply restarts the election when it fires. randomTimeout(v) returns v plus a random fraction of v, so a campaign begins in [H, 2H). Correspondingly ValidateConfig requires LeaderLeaseTimeout <= HeartbeatTimeout <= ElectionTimeout, and the leader's own lease check runs every max(minCheckInterval, LeaderLeaseTimeout - maxDiff) with minCheckInterval = 10ms, stepping down at the first check whose voter contact age exceeds LeaderLeaseTimeout; the comparison is diff <= leaseTimeout, so a check at exactly the boundary still counts the voter as contacted.\\\",\n \\\"conditions\\\":\\\"The default follower and leader loops in this snapshot, with pre-vote enabled or disabled; the numbers quoted are for a configuration accepted by ValidateConfig, and the exposure analysis assumes in-memory or low-latency RPCs.\\\",\n \\\"source_ids\\\":[\\\"follower_election_trigger\\\",\\\"follower_campaign_logic\\\",\\\"lease_check_granularity\\\",\\\"random_timeout\\\",\\\"config_validation\\\",\\\"lease_check\\\",\\\"candidate_retry\\\"]}\n\nd={\\\"rationale\\\":(\\\"Corrected the verify question's timing premise rather than leaving a vague one on record. The account previously attributed the successor-overlap condition to a leader stall or a window of ElectionTimeout - LeaderLeaseTimeout; reading the loops shows ElectionTimeout governs only a candidate's retry, the follower's election trigger is HeartbeatTimeout with randomization to [H, 2H), and the leader's lease check granularity is max(10ms, LeaderLeaseTimeout - maxDiff). Under the documented defaults the leader therefore leaves office about 500ms before the earliest campaign, and the windows can only touch when LeaderLeaseTimeout == HeartbeatTimeout, which validation permits, where the boundary comparison (diff <= leaseTimeout) and the 10ms check floor open an exposure of about 10ms plus the election itself. The question's evidence, executions and defect claim are unchanged; this update corrects the limitation and the gap answer and records the trigger semantics as a reusable Behaviour.\\\"),\n \\\"sources\\\":[\n  {\\\"id\\\":\\\"follower_election_trigger\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"config.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":149,\\\"end_line\\\":155,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"},\n  {\\\"id\\\":\\\"follower_campaign_logic\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"raft.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":222,\\\"end_line\\\":252,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"},\n  {\\\"id\\\":\\\"lease_check_granularity\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"raft.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":933,\\\"end_line\\\":946,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"},\n  {\\\"id\\\":\\\"random_timeout\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"util.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":29,\\\"end_line\\\":35,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"},\n  {\\\"id\\\":\\\"candidate_retry\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"raft.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":308,\\\"end_line\\\":318,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"}],\n \\\"knowledge\\\":[knowledge],\n \\\"interpretations\\\":[interp]}\njson.dump(d,open('update.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False); open('update.json','a').write('\\\\n')\nPY\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-15_12-22-18-hashicorp_raft-real-run --submission update.json 2>&1 | tail -3\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
